# W44-103 terminal.png per-region + per-strategy SSIM2 attribution
# Bench: encode terminal.png at d=4 with effort 5/6/7/8/9 (5 cells × 2 encoders)
# Date: 2026-05-19
# Image: /home/lilith/work/codec-corpus/gb82-sc/terminal.png (1646x1062 RGB8 screenshot)
# Ours: cjxl-rs (commit c1d699e2, W44-102 main)
# cjxl: libjxl v0.12.0 with W44-76 per-block dump patch (`libjxl--w44-76-per-block-debug-dump`)
#
# 3x3 spatial region grid; region indices (ry, rx) where (0,0)=top-left:
#   top: r00 r01 r02       (each region: 544x320 px, aligned to DCT32)
#   mid: r10 r11 r12       (mid-right + bot-right get image-edge remainder)
#   bot: r20 r21 r22
# region_w = (1646/3) & !31 = 544
# region_h = (1062/3) & !31 = 320
# Bottom row absorbs h=422 (320 + remainder 102)
# Right col absorbs w=558 (544 + remainder 14)
#
# Per-block strategy dumps for both encoders written to:
#   /tmp/w44_103_dumps/e{effort}_d4_ours/per_block_ours.tsv
#   /tmp/w44_103_dumps/e{effort}_d4_cjxl/per_block_libjxl.tsv
#
# Analysis: benchmarks/w44_103_terminal_ssim2_analysis_2026-05-19.txt
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# KEY FINDINGS
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# 1. Bimodal SSIM2 loss pattern:
#
#    e5: bytes -3.81%  global ssim2 -5.38
#    e6: bytes -2.50%  global ssim2 -5.29
#    e7: bytes -7.07%  global ssim2 -4.62
#    e8: bytes -49.90% global ssim2 -5.57
#    e9: bytes -49.79% global ssim2 -5.59
#
#    At e5/e6/e7 byte savings are small and SSIM2 loss is distributed across
#    all 9 regions (~-3 to -6). At e8/e9 our buttloop wins 50% bytes but the
#    SSIM2 loss is concentrated in the mid row (left -9.4, center -8.5)
#    while bottom-right is fine (-0.7).
#
# 2. DCT16X16 over-firing at ALL efforts:
#
#    Effort 5: ours 4870 fb  vs  cjxl  195 fb  (25x over-fire)
#    Effort 6: ours 4867 fb  vs  cjxl  195 fb
#    Effort 7: ours 5413 fb  vs  cjxl  287 fb
#    Effort 8: ours 5413 fb  vs  cjxl  287 fb  (no buttloop-induced change)
#    Effort 9: ours 5413 fb  vs  cjxl  287 fb
#
#    Strategy agreement is only 16-25%. Dominant disagreement at e7+:
#      10432 cells: ours DCT16X16  →  cjxl DCT64X64
#       3328 cells: ours DCT16X16  →  cjxl DCT32X64
#       2440 cells: ours DCT16X16  →  cjxl DCT64X32
#       2012 cells: ours DCT16X16  →  cjxl DCT16X32
#       1208 cells: ours DCT16X16  →  cjxl DCT32X32
#
#    DCT16X16 smears 16x16 pixels — fine on smooth content, catastrophic on
#    screen text (sharp glyph edges become ringy blobs). cjxl uses LARGE
#    transforms (DCT64) on the smooth screen background and SMALL transforms
#    (DCT8/AFV) on text — we use DCT16X16 for both.
#
# 3. DCT64 family entirely absent from our output:
#
#    Effort 5:  ours 0+0+0  vs  cjxl 161+85+112 (DCT64X64+DCT64X32+DCT32X64)
#    Effort 6:  ours 0+0+0  vs  cjxl 161+85+112
#    Effort 7:  ours 0+0+0  vs  cjxl 163+92+114  (try_dct64 gate at e>=7 doesn't help)
#    Effort 8:  ours 0+0+0  vs  cjxl 163+92+114
#    Effort 9:  ours 0+0+0  vs  cjxl 163+92+114
#
#    NOTE: even at e>=7 where `try_dct64=true`, we emit 0 DCT64 first-blocks.
#    The DCT64 search runs but always loses — likely because our DCT64 entropy
#    model is biased against it on high-contrast screenshot content (DCT64
#    IDCT roundtrip error on sharp text edges is enormous). cjxl picks DCT64
#    specifically on the SMOOTH bottom-row regions where there's nothing but
#    blank background — exactly where it's free of perceptual cost.
#
#    W44-93 ruled out widening the try_dct64 effort gate (regresses photos).
#    The fix here is NOT widening the gate — it's making the DCT64 search
#    WIN on smooth screen content.
#
# 4. DCT32 family also missing at e5/e6:
#
#    Effort 5: ours 0+0+0  vs  cjxl 43+97+290 (DCT32X32+DCT32X16+DCT16X32)
#    Effort 6: ours 0+0+0  vs  cjxl 43+104+296
#    Effort 7: ours 0+0+0  vs  cjxl 79+155+318  (try_dct32 ON yet 0 picked)
#
#    Same pattern: cjxl picks DCT32* on smooth blocks, we don't.
#
# 5. E8/E9 buttloop divergence — cjxl's loop produces 16x finer qac:
#
#    Per-strategy mean_qac at e8 (ours / cjxl):
#      IDENTITY:    6.12 / 99.12  (16.2x)
#      DCT8:        6.05 / 92.50  (15.3x)
#      AFV0:        6.06 / 93.24  (15.4x)
#      AFV1:        6.00 / 95.49  (15.9x)
#      AFV2:        6.16 / 83.40  (13.5x)
#      DCT16X16:    5.20 / 86.34  (16.6x)
#      DCT16X8:     5.58 / 82.15  (14.7x)
#      DCT16X32:    0    / 57.16  (∞)
#      DCT32X32:    0    / 63.86  (∞)
#      DCT64X64:    0    / 10.52  (∞; left low intentionally on smooth content)
#
#    Per-region mean qac (ours / cjxl) at e8:
#      top:  5.5/70.0   5.6/80.7   5.2/64.3
#      mid:  5.8/97.4   6.0/89.2   5.6/83.1   ← cjxl heavily refines text
#      bot:  5.0/54.4   5.1/33.3   4.9/ 7.2   ← cjxl LOWERS qac in blank area
#
#    cjxl's buttloop pushes qac much higher on text regions to preserve
#    glyph fidelity. The DECREASE in qac at R[2,2] (cjxl 7.2 vs ours 4.9)
#    is interesting: cjxl uses DCT64 + low qac on the blank area because
#    spatial coverage matters more than fidelity there. We use DCT16X16
#    + slightly higher qac everywhere.
#
#    The deviation bounds in butteraugli_loop.rs (qf_higher) likely cap our
#    qf growth too tightly. libjxl uses the same formula but the
#    initial_qf_max input differs because of adaptive_quant calibration on
#    high-mask1x1-median (screenshot) inputs.
#
# 6. Per-region SSIM2 cost concentration matches strategy errors:
#
#    Worst regions across efforts:
#      e5: R[1,0]=-5.26  R[2,2]=-5.22  (mid-left + bot-right)
#      e6: R[1,0]=-5.78  R[2,2]=-5.71
#      e7: R[2,1]=-6.10  R[0,1]=-5.80
#      e8: R[1,0]=-9.36  R[1,1]=-8.37  (mid is destroyed)
#      e9: R[1,0]=-9.36  R[1,1]=-8.45
#
#    e5/e6 mid-left and bot-right losses correspond to text-heavy regions
#    where DCT16X16 over-firing destroys glyphs. e8/e9 mid catastrophe is
#    qac under-refinement in the buttloop.
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# RANKED W44-104 ATTACK CANDIDATES
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Candidate A (HIGHEST EV, MEDIUM RISK):
#   Investigate why our DCT64/DCT32 search NEVER wins on screen-content
#   smooth regions. cjxl picks DCT64X64/DCT32X64 ~370 times per cell on
#   bot-row regions; we pick 0. Likely culprits:
#     - Our entropy_mul64 reference values (2.25, 2.25) might be too high
#       for screen-content smooth regions (text + blank mix)
#     - find_best_64x64_transform may exit early due to the IDENTITY/DCT2X2
#       merge-back path winning at the partial-merge stage
#     - The mask1x1 field for blank screen areas is very HIGH → tighter quant
#       in cost model → DCT8 wins via per-pixel loss term
#   Expected EV: 5-10 SSIM2 points on terminal e5..e7 by switching DCT16X16
#   on smooth regions to DCT64X64. NO impact on bytes either way (cjxl is
#   AT byte parity at e5/e6 — bytes are SHIPPED there).
#   Time: 2-3 days (debug, A/B, hash-lock).
#   Risk: MEDIUM — needs content-aware gate so we don't regress photo cells.
#
# Candidate B (HIGHEST EV at e8/e9, LOW-MEDIUM RISK):
#   Investigate why our butteraugli loop produces qac=5-6 globally vs cjxl's
#   qac=70-97 on text regions. Likely culprits:
#     - K_BUTTERAUGLI_ACCEPT_FACTOR = 1.05 lets us "good enough" exit too
#       early on screenshot content where text contrast pushes butteraugli
#       score way above target distance
#     - Deviation bound qf_higher computed from initial_qf_max may be tighter
#       on our adaptive_quant (post-W44-86 fixes pushed initial quant down on
#       flat screen content)
#     - Multi-seed search at e10+ might find a higher-qac solution; the
#       e8/e9 single-seed loop converges on the cheap one
#   Expected EV: 5-7 SSIM2 points at e8/e9 (matching cjxl's behavior) AND
#   potentially +50% bytes (which IS the right tradeoff — cjxl's choice; we
#   shouldn't be 50% cheaper at -5 SSIM2 vs cjxl).
#   Time: 2-3 days.
#   Risk: LOW — changes only affect e8/e9 (buttloop gate).
#
# Candidate C (NARROW SCOPE, LOW RISK):
#   Use the existing `screenshot_suppressed` entropy_mul table (already
#   shipped via W22-1 / W44-65 opt-in) as a default for terminal-class
#   content. Currently default-off; the gate fires on median(mask1x1) > 95.
#   Terminal.png has mask1x1 around 100-120 (very high).
#   This would lift IDENTITY (1.04→1.85), DCT2X2 (0.95→1.15), AFV (0.82→0.95),
#   DCT4X8 (0.86→0.98) — suppressing the small-block over-selection that
#   DOES happen in our mid-row data.
#   Expected EV: 1-2 SSIM2 points (helps but doesn't address DCT16X16
#   over-firing root cause).
#   Time: 1 day.
#   Risk: LOW (already opt-in tested).
#
# Candidate D (LOW EV, INVESTIGATIVE):
#   Audit our DCT16X16 selection criterion. We pick 4870 DCT16X16 vs cjxl 195.
#   The split-vs-keep decision at the 16x16 level must be biased. Check
#   libjxl `find_best_16x16_transform` vs ours — likely the `kFavor2X2 = -0.4`
#   discount on smaller transforms isn't firing for us because we're entering
#   the 16x16 evaluation from the 32x32 fallback, not from the bottom-up 8x8
#   merge path.
#   Expected EV: 2-4 SSIM2 points (changes WHERE DCT16X16 fires).
#   Time: 2-3 days.
#   Risk: MEDIUM — could regress photo cells where DCT16X16 IS correct.
#
# Candidate E (DEFER):
#   Buttloop e7 promotion (already-suggested W44-101 follow-on). The e7
#   SSIM2 -4.6 IS smaller than e5/e6 -5.3, suggesting some refinement benefit
#   even without buttloop. Adding buttloop at e7 would compound that. But
#   e8/e9 with buttloop ALREADY underperforms (B is the prior). Defer until
#   B closes the e8/e9 buttloop bug.
#
# Recommendation: ship A and B in parallel. They address different efforts
# (A for e5..e7, B for e8/e9). Both will likely need a "terminal-class"
# content discriminator (similar to W44-91/96 zenanalyze pattern, gated on
# median(mask1x1) > 95 + low fcbr).
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# DO NOT
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# - DO NOT widen try_dct64 effort gate (W44-93 RULED OUT — regresses photos)
# - DO NOT widen cfl_two_pass to e5/e6 (W44-102 RULED OUT — regresses quality)
# - DO NOT respawn FMA precision claims (per W44-66 user correction)
# - DO NOT default-flip content_aware_entropy_mul without first investigating
#   why DCT16X16/DCT64 are at fundamental disagreement with cjxl
# - DO NOT touch tokenize_ac_coefficients / nz_context (W44-75 bit-exact)
#
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# REPRODUCER
# ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
#   # Run the global + per-region SSIM2 bench (writes the main .tsv)
#   cargo run -p jxl-encoder --release \
#     --features 'parallel butteraugli-loop ssim2-loop' \
#     --example w44_103_terminal_ssim2_dump
#
#   # Run per-cell W44-76 strategy dumps (writes /tmp/w44_103_dumps/)
#   benchmarks/w44_103_run_dumps.sh
#
#   # Run analysis (cross-references regions × strategies × qac)
#   python3 benchmarks/w44_103_analyze.py > \
#     benchmarks/w44_103_terminal_ssim2_analysis_2026-05-19.txt
#
# Files written:
#   benchmarks/w44_103_terminal_ssim2_dump_2026-05-19.tsv     (main bench)
#   benchmarks/w44_103_terminal_ssim2_dump_2026-05-19.meta    (this file)
#   benchmarks/w44_103_terminal_ssim2_analysis_2026-05-19.txt (analysis output)
#   benchmarks/w44_103_run_dumps.sh                           (dump runner)
#   benchmarks/w44_103_analyze.py                             (analysis script)
